In positive-unlabeled learning, training data consists of instances with known positive labels, but uncertain negative labels. Current approaches to positive-unlabeled learning are limited by their reliance on a single loss function, which often overlooks the complexities of the training process. To address this limitation, we introduce the ImPULSeS framework, a novel approach that utilizes a self-supervised training method to learn a classifier. This framework leverages a dual-contrastive loss function to learn a feature representation that distinguishes positive instances, and a classification loss to predict the class labels. As a result, ImPULSeS achieves superior performance compared to existing positive-unlabeled learning methods, as demonstrated on multiple real-world datasets. Notably, ImPULSeS outperforms state-of-the-art positive-unlabeled learning methods by 5-10% on average across various benchmark datasets. The proposed framework also generalizes well to unseen test data, outperforming previous approaches by 2-5% on average. The implementation details are available on GitHub at https://github.com/chaosekang/ImPULSeS. This code release also includes a simple tool for generating synthetic positive-unlabeled learning datasets. The implementation is accessible at https://github.com/chaosekang/ImPULSeS. The code was developed using Python 3.9.2 and can be run on Google Colab without requiring any software installation. The trained models can be downloaded from the specified link. This code release is free to use and distribute. The implementation details are provided in the supplementary material. The paper's arXiv link is available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.13051. The paper's bibtex entry is provided as follows:  \begin{verbatim}
