A novel framework, Out-of-distribution Novel Class Detection, aims to identify novel classes in datasets where samples are imbalanced and unseen. A proposed solution, Out-of-distribution Novel Class Generation (OPG), uses Generative Adversarial Networks to create artificial samples from known classes, which are then combined with real samples from novel classes to produce pseudo-unseen data. This approach allows for the detection of unknown classes in a balanced and efficient manner. Through extensive evaluation on various datasets, including imbalanced ones, OPG demonstrates its effectiveness in detecting novel classes, outperforming state-of-the-art methods while requiring minimal hyperparameter tuning. Moreover, OPG achieves superior performance when compared to other approaches, including those that employ data augmentation techniques. This approach also shows significant improvements in performance when compared to other approaches, with an average AUC score of 0.94, which is a substantial 23% higher than the highest score achieved by 2020 OC-Detection challenge winners. All the source code and models used in this research are publicly available at the specified GitHub repository. For more information about the challenge, please visit its related website. The results of this research were accepted for publication in the 2021 ICML workshop on Novelty Detection and Description. Due to space limitations, the actual paper is not fully included here; please refer to the actual paper for the complete results and methodology. Due to space limitations, the actual paper is not fully included here; please refer to the actual paper for the complete results and methodology. \end{document} 
